rabex

This crate was written after reading the source of rabex
(https://github.com/jakobhellermann/RustyAssetBundleEXtractor, Copyright Rudolf Kolbe and Jakob
Hellermann, MIT OR Apache-2.0), at revision 56dd8a4d8d5048b6ee7664eadc89d98343c66002 of the
patodois fork. What was read there, with the file of this crate that does the same job:

- the bundle header, block and directory reading (src/bundle.rs);
- the serialized file reader (src/serialized.rs);
- the type tree blob reader, and the classification of type tree nodes into kinds
  (src/typetree.rs);
- the serde deserializer: its dispatch over kinds, its field-name, map-key and typeless-byte
  helper deserializers, and the behaviour that the deserializer was checked to share (src/de.rs);
- read_ext.rs (this crate reads from slices, through src/cursor.rs);
- the Unity version parser (this crate keeps the version as the string in the file);
- the common-strings and class-id tables (src/common_strings.rs, src/class_id.rs).

The layouts were learned from rabex, and the classification of nodes and the shape of the
deserializer follow its structure. No text or expressions were pasted.

Because the structure of those parts follows rabex, treat them as derived from it: they are distributed under the same terms as rabex (MIT OR Apache-2.0), copyright Rudolf Kolbe and Jakob Hellermann for what derives from their work, and the permission notices in LICENSE-MIT and LICENSE-APACHE apply to them.

AssetStudio

The two tables were then checked entry by entry against rabex's and against AssetStudio's (MIT,
Perfare), which was read for that; tools/check_tables.py repeats the comparison. Nothing was
copied.

README.md, "Provenance", states exactly what was read.
